> [!answer]
> The nurse lacked:
> - **Respect for the patient and for his autonomy** — she dismissed a legitimate concern about his own body and treated him with contempt;
> - **Good communication and listening skills**, and **courtesy/empathy (compassion)**;
> - **Beneficence and non-maleficence** — her conduct harmed rather than helped the patient;
> - **Competence and accountability** — she acted outside the documented surgical instruction and would not accept correction;
> - **Humility, patience and self-control (emotional intelligence)** — she responded with anger;
> - **Integrity, trustworthiness and professionalism** generally, including responsibility for her own actions.

> [!answer]
> **Areas of conflict**
> 1. **Between the health team and the patient or family** — over the goals of treatment: insistence on futile treatment or on discharge against advice, refusal of blood transfusion or surgery on religious grounds, refusal of consent for a child, demands for an inappropriate drug or test, disagreement about disclosure of a diagnosis to the patient, or about end-of-life and resuscitation decisions.
> 2. **Within the health team (inter- and intra-professional)** — doctors, nurses, laboratory scientists, pharmacists and management disagreeing over diagnosis or management plan, over professional boundaries and scope of practice, over hierarchy, duty rosters, conditions of service and remuneration.
> 3. **Between clinicians and management/administration** — rationing of beds, theatre time, reagents and staff; cost-containment versus clinical need.
> 4. **Between the hospital and the wider community or payer** — billing, insurance refusals, cultural expectations.
> 5. **Conflicts of value within the practitioner** — dual loyalty, conscientious objection.
>
> **Management of conflict**
> - **Open, early and respectful communication**, with active listening to all parties, and a formal **family/team meeting** at which goals of care are agreed;
> - **Clarify the facts and the clinical picture first**, then the values and interests of each side; identify common ground (the patient's welfare);
> - **Negotiation and mediation** by a neutral senior colleague, and referral to the **hospital ethics committee** for difficult cases;
> - **Use of policies, protocols and codes of conduct**, and a functioning **grievance/complaints procedure** and disciplinary process for staff disputes;
> - **Patient advocates, interpreters, chaplains and social workers** to bridge cultural and religious gaps;
> - **Documentation** of discussions and decisions, with a second opinion where appropriate, and recourse to legal process or the courts only as a last resort;
> - **Prevention** — team-building, clear job descriptions and lines of reporting, staff training in communication and conflict resolution.
